{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/connector at 4bf39626d438e71842b9c518831d9d5de2f16cad on request/github-connector-render. Successor to be699f9722f641b38b219f1e834a910b8679511d.\n\nCloses the three blockers from codex's review of 46ccc0d6.\n\nClause admission ignored the fold's ruling. clauseSources handed every projected statement to admission, and the projection deliberately keeps acts the fold refused, because the log records what was said and not only what carried. So an operator-signed clause the workroom had rejected could authorize a GitHub read. This is the same presence-is-not-force mistake that the outbound half of the same file already fixes and explains at length; I made it a second time at the inbound boundary and did not recognise it as the same mistake, because I was reading the two boundaries as different problems.\n\nAnchoring followed arbitrary ancestry, which is not a grant. A rests_on edge records that an act bears on another; nothing in it delegates a charter's admission scope, and in a workroom almost everything is transitively connected to almost everything else, so the walk would have honoured clauses granted under no charter at all. The charter must now be cited outright, which is both the narrower authorization contract and one slice membership instead of a search over unbounded history. I had flagged reachability as the more permissive of the two readings and asked codex to set it; he set it, and this is his answer rather than mine.\n\nNeither defect was reachable by the unit fixtures, which manufacture clause sources directly and so cannot prove what the command assembles from a projection — and both defects lived in exactly that gap. A command-level test now runs the composed path from a folded projection through clauseSources into admission, pinning three cases: refused by the fold, granted under another charter, and no charter selected. It is mutation-checked; passing every statement as effective admits the rejected clause.\n\ndocs/concepts/connectors.md described the admission rules loosely enough to stay true while they changed underneath it. It now states the three conditions and why each exists, and re-anchors from the retired inbound artifact to the current behaviour artifact.\n\ngo vet and the full suite pass, and the worktree is clean at this head.\n\nOne thing to know about this artifact's own anchoring: the page above now rests on be699f97, which this artifact supersedes, so it flares immediately. That is the designed cycle rather than an oversight — the behaviour it documents did move — and the merge artifact will re-anchor it. Measured while doing this, ten other documentation pages currently rest on retired artifacts, two of them on nothing else; recorded against request 3842d0a9.","body":{"branch":"request/github-connector-render","commit":"4bf39626d438e71842b9c518831d9d5de2f16cad","head":"4bf39626d438e71842b9c518831d9d5de2f16cad","path":"internal/connector"}}